iq-js-02.answer:
- A closure lets a function access the bindings of its lexical environment, even after the outer function has returned.
- It is useful for private counters, callbacks and function factories
- captured bindings are live, not automatically frozen copies.

- Every factory call has its own binding.
- The first closure remembers the updated total
- not a frozen copy of zero.
- So two calls of a increase one total, while b's counter starts separately.

```js
function makeTracker() {
  let minutes = 0;
  return amount => (minutes += amount);
}
const a = makeTracker(), b = makeTracker();
console.log(a(5), a(3), b(2)); // 5, 8, 2
```

iq-js-03.answer:
- A regular function's this is decided at the call site: an object method call, an explicit call/apply/bind, a constructor call or a plain call.
- An arrow function does not create its own this
- it uses the this of the surrounding lexical scope, so bind does not replace its this.

- An arrow has no arguments binding of its own and cannot be called with new.
- When this should come from the receiver of the invocation, use a regular function.
- In the example call changes the this of normal, not the lexical this of the arrow.

```js
const lesson = {
  minutes: 12,
  callbacks() {
    return {normal: function () { return this.minutes; }, arrow: () => this.minutes};
  }
};
const callbacks = lesson.callbacks();
console.log(callbacks.normal.call({minutes:99})); // 99
console.log(callbacks.arrow.call({minutes:99})); // 12
```

iq-js-13.answer:
- function* lets the body pause/resume
- at a yield control returns to the caller, and on a next() call execution resumes from that point.
- It is useful for producing lazy sequences, custom iterables and large/infinite data chunk by chunk, without building the whole result in an array up front.

- A generator call creates an iterator
- the body does not run immediately.
- next() resumes it up to the next yield or return.
- for...of takes the yielded values, not the final return value.
- A generator does not create parallel or asynchronous execution by itself.

```js
function* lessonIds() { yield 10; yield 20; return 30; }
const ids = lessonIds();
console.log(ids.next()); // {value:10, done:false}
console.log(ids.next()); // {value:20, done:false}
console.log(ids.next()); // {value:30, done:true}
```

iq-react-05.answer:
- A controlled input's value comes from React state and onChange updates that state.
- An uncontrolled input keeps its current value in the DOM and is accessed through defaultValue or a ref
- avoid switching one input between modes during its lifecycle.

- Keep a controlled input's value and change handler consistent.
- In an uncontrolled input defaultValue gives only the initial value
- the DOM manages the typing after that.
- Do not switch the same input from uncontrolled to controlled or the reverse.
- In the example the first reads from state, the second from a ref on submit.

```jsx
import {useRef, useState} from 'react';
function Controlled() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');
  return <input aria-label="Controlled name" value={name} onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)}/>;
}
function Uncontrolled() {
  const input = useRef(null);
  return <form onSubmit={e => {e.preventDefault(); console.log(input.current.value);}}>
    <input aria-label="Uncontrolled name" ref={input} defaultValue="Learner"/><button>Read</button>
  </form>;
}
```

iq-react-06.answer:
- memo is an optimization that skips rendering a component on unchanged props
- useMemo caches a calculation result and useCallback caches a function identity.
- These are not correctness tools: profile the actual rendering cost, and unstable object props or context updates can make the cache benefit disappear.

- useMemo caches a calculated result
- useCallback caches a function identity
- memo can skip a render caused by the parent when the props are equal.
- A stable identity is not a substitute for correctness.
- Measure the relevant workload: state/context updates and unstable inputs can still cause work.
- In a compiler-enabled build the need for manual memoization can be lower.

```jsx
import {memo, useCallback, useMemo, useState} from 'react';
const Results = memo(function Results({items, onChoose}) {
  return <ul>{items.map(x => <li key={x.id}><button onClick={() => onChoose(x.id)}>{x.title}</button></li>)}</ul>;
});
function Search({items, query}) {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(null);
  const visible = useMemo(() => items.filter(x => x.title.includes(query)), [items, query]);
  const choose = useCallback(id => setSelected(id), []);
  return <><Results items={visible} onChoose={choose}/><p>{selected}</p></>;
}
```

iq-react-07.answer:
- Keep state with the closest owner first
- lift it for shared consumers and consider Context for widely needed values.
- A store can be useful for frequent granular updates, selectors or complex cross-feature workflows
- do not blindly mix the server cache with client-only UI state.

- A consumer reads the value of the nearest matching provider.
- Context distributes a value and updates the consumers on a change
- it does not define a reducer, middleware or a server-cache policy.
- Before adding a shared-state mechanism, see whether composition can reduce the prop drilling.

```jsx
import {createContext, useContext, useState} from 'react';
const ThemeContext = createContext('light');
function Label() { return <p>{useContext(ThemeContext)}</p>; }
function App() {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');
  return <ThemeContext.Provider value={theme}><button onClick={() => setTheme(t => t === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light')}>Toggle</button><Label/></ThemeContext.Provider>;
}
```

iq-react-13.answer:
- A custom hook extracts stateful logic from a component, but every calling component gets its own independent state instance — no shared state is created automatically.
- A reusable hook exposes a clear contract (what input it takes, what it returns) and manages its internal dependencies/cleanup itself.

- It shares client hook behaviour, not one global state cell.
- Every useOnline call has its own state/effect.
- navigator.onLine is only a connectivity hint, not proof that the API is reachable.
- In server rendering the initial snapshot and hydration have to be kept deliberately consistent.

```jsx
import {useEffect, useState} from 'react';
function useOnline() {
  const [online, setOnline] = useState(() => typeof navigator === 'undefined' ? true : navigator.onLine);
  useEffect(() => {
    const sync = () => setOnline(navigator.onLine);
    sync();
    window.addEventListener('online', sync); window.addEventListener('offline', sync);
    return () => {window.removeEventListener('online', sync); window.removeEventListener('offline', sync);};
  }, []);
  return online;
}
```

iq-added-html-events.answer:
- Inline event attributes are possible
- addEventListener keeps the behaviour separate and supports multiple listeners and cleanup.
- Use a semantic control and register the listener after the element exists.
- Inspect the action from the event object.
