iq-added-react-rerender-triggers.answer:
- Its own state, normally a parent render, consumed context and a subscribed external store can trigger the work.
- Props come from the parent's new render; a child does not mutate them independently.
- A skip is possible through an equal-state bailout/memoization.
- Mount creates the identity, update preserves/replaces it, unmount removes it and cleans up.
- In the example the cleanup of the old committed id runs, then the new setup
- it is not an exact replacement for every historical class lifecycle.

