iq-added-dsa-intervals.answer:
- Sort the intervals by start
- maintain the last merged interval.
- If they overlap under the chosen endpoint rule extend the end, otherwise append.
- Closed [1,3]/[3,5] overlap
- define the policy for half-open touching ranges deliberately.
- Assume a finite start<=end.
- The example does not mutate the input
- O(n log n) time/O(n) storage.
- Test the empty, nested, touching and disjoint cases.

```js
function mergeClosed(intervals) {
  const sorted = intervals.map(x => [...x]).sort((a,b) => a[0]-b[0]);
  const result = [];
  for (const [start,end] of sorted) {
    const last = result.at(-1);
    if (last && start <= last[1]) last[1] = Math.max(last[1], end);
    else result.push([start,end]);
  }
  return result;
}
```

iq-added-dsa-prefix-count.answer:
- The difference between the current prefix s and an earlier prefix s-target gives a target subarray.
- The same prefix can occur multiple times, so keep frequencies.
- An initial zero with frequency one represents a start at the empty prefix.
- Count the matches before inserting the current prefix, otherwise an empty subarray can be counted for a zero target.
- Expected O(n) time/space with normal hashing
- respect the exact-range limits of Number.

```js
function countTarget(nums, target) {
  const seen = new Map([[0,1]]);
  let prefix = 0, count = 0;
  for (const n of nums) {
    prefix += n;
    count += seen.get(prefix-target) ?? 0;
    seen.set(prefix, (seen.get(prefix) ?? 0)+1);
  }
  return count;
}
console.log(countTarget([1,-1,1], 1)); // 3
```

